# Initialize Git 

Git is a version control system which helps users track files remotely so that its easier to collaborate. GitHub is the platform that helps users execute all the features of Git - quite similar to R and Rstudio as an example

*Please note that you must login to your GitHub account to follow this step*

## Open RStudio terminal

![](SS_terminal.png)

In Rstudio *Terminal* use the following commands 
- git init *Git starts tracking this folder*
- git add . *Files now staged*
- git commit -m "Message" *File is now saved*

## Create a repository in GitHub

- Create a new remote repository
![](SS_git1.png)

- Name it, make it public and add a README

![](SS_git2.png)

- Copy the HTTPS Link

![](SS_git3.png)

## Use the repository link and follow the commands in the Terminal
- git remote add origin *https://github.com/...*
- git push -u origin main *or master depending on what the original branch is called in your device*

Now the project is linked with a remote which will help us collaborate and use the features of the Git repository 

# Create a new branch 

Branches are used to safely experiment and develop features of the main project

In Rstudio *Terminal* use the following commands to create a new branch *testbranch*
- git branch testbranch *Creates a branch*
- git switch testbranch *Switches to the new branch*

## Make changes to example.qmd, commit and push

Remember to use git push origin *testbranch* to push because these changes are being saved into the branch we just created. So what happens to main? It remains unchanged, which is why working in branches help you solve different problems without bothering your main project file

## Add a new folder 
In Rstudio *Terminal* use the following commands 
- mkdir data
- git add data/
- git commit -m "Data folder added"

*Ohh no! Did we just forget to add the files we needed in this folder? This is a quick fix!* 

## Bring the files and amend the last commit 
- Copy and paste a .csv file to the newly created data folder

In Rstudio *Terminal* use the following commands 
- git commit --amend

This will open vim which helps you change the commit message you just amended 

- Press 'i'  which takes you to insert mode
- Change commit message ' Data folder and file added ' 
- Press ' esc ' button and type ':wq' to save and exit 

## Push 
In Rstudio *Terminal* use the following commands 
- git push --force 

We are using force push because we are rewriting Git history 

# Simulate a conflict on Main branch

When collaborating, conflicts are a common scenario - this usually happens when two branches change the same part of a file. In this step we will demonstrate how to resolve such conflicts

In Rstudio *Terminal* use the following commands 
- git checkout main

## Make changes to example.qmd
- Type the following on the first line underneath the heading 

*A line added for conflict*

In Rstudio *Terminal* use the following commands 
- git add example.qmd
- git commit -m "Line added for conflict"
- git push origin main 

## Merge the branches and resolve conflict 

In Rstudio *Terminal* use the following commands 

- git merge testbranch

